    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Chinese Brand AITO Plans 2027 European Launch\nNew Hot Wheels RLC Exclusive Model Is an RWB Porsche That You Probably Can’t Buy\n17 photos\nPhoto: Mattel Creations\nI spend way too much time looking at Hot Wheels and other diecast listings or auctions. It’s a mix of wasting time, finding some bargain deals, and learning about the market. For instances, prices for Red Line Club collectibles in my area are now half of what they were like only six months ago.\nPeople are now trying to sell their “special” items for the same money Elite 64 cars were trading hands when the year started. I don’t imagine the market can go any lower than this, unless the economy crashes altogether.\nDespite all this, some tiny cars are seemingly unaffected and you know which ones I’m talking about. People will still pay a premium to make sure they get that new Ferrari , or that rare Chase they wanted. And the same goes for the RWB Porsches. Mattel only has two such castings: one in Car Culture with three variations in seven years, and one in the Red Line Club that only had two variations between 2020 and 2025.\nNow try to look at how many RWB collectibles you’ll find over at Pop Race, Tarmac Works and Inno64. Mattel has fallen behind on the topic, and I wonder if it did so on purpose. After all, scarcity generates excitement, excitement generates panic, panic leads to more sales. It almost sounds like a lesson that a Sith Lord would teach you, but it’s not.\nSomeone is asking $1,250 for a First Edition release of Brendon Vetuskey RWB 930, while last year’s Spectraflame Teal version can still cost as much as $499. It doesn’t mean it’s genuinely worth that much, but people will still try to profit from the hype surrounding it.\nWhat boggles me the most is that someone seems to have already gotten his hands on the new, third variation of the casting. The asking price for the Spectraflame bright orange RWB 930 is $452.99 and you just have to ask some questions here: is this a scam?\nIs it just a custom-made replica? Or how else would someone get this days before it’s even available on Mattel Creations? It’s easier to just walk away and maybe buy a 1:18th-scale RWB collectible from Ignition Models instead.\nThat’s what I would do if I wanted to spend that kind of cash on the hobby. Anyway, this casting is returning to the 6-Spoke Real Riders wheels it started with back in 2020. You get opening doors and an opening engine hood. There’s no custom license plate, and no name on the side of the car like you’ll see with more premium brands or in the real world.\nBut if you really think it’s worth the hassle, you can try paying attention to Mattel Creations on October 20 at 9 AM PT. With any luck, you can secure one for $38 before taxes. Your best chance is to have an Overdrive account, though.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHot Wheels Red Line Club rwb Porsche porsche 930 Hot Wheels Porsche Hot Wheels RWB\nAbout the author: Dragos Chitulescu\nThe things Dragos enjoys the most in life are, in no particular order: cars, motorcycles, diecast cars, and drifting. He's seen (and driven) many vehicles since he started his writing career back in 2009, but his garage currently houses a 1991 Mazda RX-7 FC3S Turbo II and a 1999 Suzuki SV650-S. Full profile\nUP NEXT The Cuorerosso Shooting Brake Is THE Alfa Romeo We Need – Rendering",
